{"reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.595 with oscillation, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis of high variance and suboptimal generalization. The historical experiment with 'drop_last=True' successfully fixed a data loading bug, which we also implemented (already in current code). To reduce variance and improve robustness, we must modify training_evaluation to implement 5-fold cross-validation ensemble, gradient clipping, and SWA. Additionally, training_evaluation is the appropriate module to add inference-time TTA. Model_design is not changed because the architecture already works; we only modify training strategy and inference pipeline. Data_processing_and_feature_engineering is not changed because features are already comprehensive and the CV split is handled within training_evaluation.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Replace the single 85/15 split with 5-fold cross-validation: for each fold, train a model on 4 folds and validate on the holdout, saving the best model per fold. Modify the training loop to include gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0) after loss.backward() but before optimizer.step(). Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) by saving model checkpoints from the last 5 epochs and averaging their weights instead of using the single best model per fold. For inference, implement Test-Time Augmentation (TTA) by generating 3 augmented versions per test sample (original, 5% word dropout, rare word synonym replacement) and averaging softmax probabilities. The final submission is the average of predictions across all 5 folds and all TTA versions. This directly implements the initial plan's three main changes, validated by similar historical experiments showing that batch handling fixes (drop_last) and ensemble methods reliably improve log-loss."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show validation log-loss plateauing around 0.595 with oscillation, confirming the initial plan's diagnosis of high variance and suboptimal generalization. The historical experiment with 'drop_last=True' successfully fixed a data loading bug, which we also implemented (already in current code). To reduce variance and improve robustness, we must modify training_evaluation to implement 5-fold cross-validation ensemble, gradient clipping, and SWA. Additionally, training_evaluation is the appropriate module to add inference-time TTA. Model_design is not changed because the architecture already works; we only modify training strategy and inference pipeline. Data_processing_and_feature_engineering is not changed because features are already comprehensive and the CV split is handled within training_evaluation.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the single 85/15 split with 5-fold cross-validation: for each fold, train a model on 4 folds and validate on the holdout, saving the best model per fold. Modify the training loop to include gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0) after loss.backward() but before optimizer.step(). Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) by saving model checkpoints from the last 5 epochs and averaging their weights instead of using the single best model per fold. For inference, implement Test-Time Augmentation (TTA) by generating 3 augmented versions per test sample (original, 5% word dropout, rare word synonym replacement) and averaging softmax probabilities. The final submission is the average of predictions across all 5 folds and all TTA versions. This directly implements the initial plan's three main changes, validated by similar historical experiments showing that batch handling fixes (drop_last) and ensemble methods reliably improve log-loss."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan aims to replace TF-IDF+LogisticRegression with a DistilBERT transformer for better contextual understanding. Historical experiment 1 successfully used a hybrid model with BERT and stylometric features, achieving strong performance. Experiment 2 failed due to overfitting of ModernBERT on small data. The consistent underfitting (CV logloss ~0.65) confirms the need for a transformer. I choose three modules: data_processing_and_feature_engineering to retain useful handcrafted features, model_design to implement the transformer architecture, and training_evaluation to handle the optimizer and training loop. This covers all necessary changes from the initial plan while learning from historical successes (hybrid features) and failures (avoid overfitting).", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Keep existing statistical features (char_count, word_count, punctuation rates, etc.) but integrate them with the transformer. Add tokenization using the DistilBERT tokenizer with max_length=128. Further refine features by adding author-specific word counts (e.g., words like 'eldritch', 'abyss' for Lovecraft, 'hopeless', 'gloom' for Poe) based on historical success. WHY: Handcrafted features complement transformer embeddings, as shown in historical experiment 1, improving accuracy. The initial plan's 'Keep unchanged' list allows this. HOW: Use HuggingFace 'BERT-base-uncased' tokenizer to tokenize text, then extract statistical features from the raw text. The feature engineering function will be called in a preprocessing pipeline before training.", "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the LogisticRegression with a DistilBERT sequence classification model (bert-base-uncased) with a dropout layer and a linear classifier head for 3 classes. Additionally, incorporate the statistical features by concatenating them with the [CLS] token embedding before the classifier head (hybrid architecture). WHY: Transformers capture context well, and adding stylometric features adds robustness, as seen in successful historical experiment 1. This avoids the overfitting of experiment 2 by using a smaller model (DistilBERT) and extra features. HOW: Use transformers library to load 'distilbert-base-uncased', add a fusion layer that combines [CLS] embedding (768 dim) with feature vector (e.g., 15 dim) -> 783 dim -> dropout -> 3 output classes. Train with label smoothing cross-entropy loss.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement training with AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01), linear warmup for first 10% steps then linear decay, batch size 16, and 3 epochs. Use mixed precision (fp16) for efficiency. Do early stopping based on validation logloss. WHY: This matches the initial plan's optimizer Change #2 and prevents overfitting (unlike historical experiment 2). HOW: Use HuggingFace Trainer API or custom training loop with torch. Track validation logloss after each epoch, save best model. Generate submission with row normalization and clipping as specified."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan aims to replace TF-IDF+LogisticRegression with a DistilBERT transformer for better contextual understanding. Historical experiment 1 successfully used a hybrid model with BERT and stylometric features, achieving strong performance. Experiment 2 failed due to overfitting of ModernBERT on small data. The consistent underfitting (CV logloss ~0.65) confirms the need for a transformer. I choose three modules: data_processing_and_feature_engineering to retain useful handcrafted features, model_design to implement the transformer architecture, and training_evaluation to handle the optimizer and training loop. This covers all necessary changes from the initial plan while learning from historical successes (hybrid features) and failures (avoid overfitting).",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Keep existing statistical features (char_count, word_count, punctuation rates, etc.) but integrate them with the transformer. Add tokenization using the DistilBERT tokenizer with max_length=128. Further refine features by adding author-specific word counts (e.g., words like 'eldritch', 'abyss' for Lovecraft, 'hopeless', 'gloom' for Poe) based on historical success. WHY: Handcrafted features complement transformer embeddings, as shown in historical experiment 1, improving accuracy. The initial plan's 'Keep unchanged' list allows this. HOW: Use HuggingFace 'BERT-base-uncased' tokenizer to tokenize text, then extract statistical features from the raw text. The feature engineering function will be called in a preprocessing pipeline before training.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the LogisticRegression with a DistilBERT sequence classification model (bert-base-uncased) with a dropout layer and a linear classifier head for 3 classes. Additionally, incorporate the statistical features by concatenating them with the [CLS] token embedding before the classifier head (hybrid architecture). WHY: Transformers capture context well, and adding stylometric features adds robustness, as seen in successful historical experiment 1. This avoids the overfitting of experiment 2 by using a smaller model (DistilBERT) and extra features. HOW: Use transformers library to load 'distilbert-base-uncased', add a fusion layer that combines [CLS] embedding (768 dim) with feature vector (e.g., 15 dim) -> 783 dim -> dropout -> 3 output classes. Train with label smoothing cross-entropy loss.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement training with AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01), linear warmup for first 10% steps then linear decay, batch size 16, and 3 epochs. Use mixed precision (fp16) for efficiency. Do early stopping based on validation logloss. WHY: This matches the initial plan's optimizer Change #2 and prevents overfitting (unlike historical experiment 2). HOW: Use HuggingFace Trainer API or custom training loop with torch. Track validation logloss after each epoch, save best model. Generate submission with row normalization and clipping as specified."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current TF-IDF + ensemble (LogisticRegression + LightGBM) achieves a validation log loss of ~0.597, which is suboptimal. Historical experiments show that transformer-based models (e.g., DistilBERT) combined with TF-IDF features significantly improve performance, reaching log loss below 0.4. However, naive transformer-only approaches can overfit due to small dataset size. The successful hybrid approach from historical experiments (TF-IDF + engineered features + calibrated classifier) suggests that combining deep contextual embeddings with strong lexical signals is robust. The initial plan proposes a hybrid of transformer + TF-IDF, which aligns with successful cases. To avoid overfitting (as seen in failed transformer-only experiments), modifications focus on data processing (feature engineering to augment TF-IDF), model design (hybrid architecture with dropout and reduced complexity), and training strategy (lower learning rates, early stopping, mixed precision). These modules are chosen as the most impactful for implementing the hybrid approach while ensuring stability.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add engineered stylometric features (character/word/sentence counts, punctuation ratios, capital ratio, unique word ratio, stopword ratio) as a separate dense feature set, concatenated with TF-IDF vectors using sparse hstack. This enriches the lexical signal and provides explicit style markers, as successful in historical experiments. Use StandardScaler for the engineered features to normalize scales. Keep the existing TF-IDF vectorization unchanged. The output will be a combined sparse matrix (TF-IDF + scaled dense features) for the model.", "model_design": "Replace the ensemble of LogisticRegression and LightGBM with a hybrid model: a pretrained DistilBERT for context embeddings, combined with the TF-IDF+engineered features. The architecture: DistilBERT outputs a pooled sentence vector (768-dim), which is concatenated with the output of a dense encoder (e.g., 2-layer MLP with dropout) applied to the TF-IDF+engineered features. The concatenated vector passes to a final softmax classifier (3 classes). Use label smoothing cross-entropy loss to improve calibration. Implement in PyTorch with HuggingFace Transformers.", "training_evaluation": "Use stratified 5-fold cross-validation with the same splits. For each fold, train the hybrid model with differential learning rates: 2e-5 for DistilBERT and 1e-3 for the classifier head and dense encoder. Use AdamW optimizer, cosine learning rate schedule with warmup (10% of steps), gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0), and early stopping on validation log loss with patience=3. Mixed precision training (fp16) for speed. Average predictions across folds and normalize row-wise to sum to 1, then clip to [1e-15, 1-1e-15] before saving submission."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current TF-IDF + ensemble (LogisticRegression + LightGBM) achieves a validation log loss of ~0.597, which is suboptimal. Historical experiments show that transformer-based models (e.g., DistilBERT) combined with TF-IDF features significantly improve performance, reaching log loss below 0.4. However, naive transformer-only approaches can overfit due to small dataset size. The successful hybrid approach from historical experiments (TF-IDF + engineered features + calibrated classifier) suggests that combining deep contextual embeddings with strong lexical signals is robust. The initial plan proposes a hybrid of transformer + TF-IDF, which aligns with successful cases. To avoid overfitting (as seen in failed transformer-only experiments), modifications focus on data processing (feature engineering to augment TF-IDF), model design (hybrid architecture with dropout and reduced complexity), and training strategy (lower learning rates, early stopping, mixed precision). These modules are chosen as the most impactful for implementing the hybrid approach while ensuring stability.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add engineered stylometric features (character/word/sentence counts, punctuation ratios, capital ratio, unique word ratio, stopword ratio) as a separate dense feature set, concatenated with TF-IDF vectors using sparse hstack. This enriches the lexical signal and provides explicit style markers, as successful in historical experiments. Use StandardScaler for the engineered features to normalize scales. Keep the existing TF-IDF vectorization unchanged. The output will be a combined sparse matrix (TF-IDF + scaled dense features) for the model.",
    "model_design": "Replace the ensemble of LogisticRegression and LightGBM with a hybrid model: a pretrained DistilBERT for context embeddings, combined with the TF-IDF+engineered features. The architecture: DistilBERT outputs a pooled sentence vector (768-dim), which is concatenated with the output of a dense encoder (e.g., 2-layer MLP with dropout) applied to the TF-IDF+engineered features. The concatenated vector passes to a final softmax classifier (3 classes). Use label smoothing cross-entropy loss to improve calibration. Implement in PyTorch with HuggingFace Transformers.",
    "training_evaluation": "Use stratified 5-fold cross-validation with the same splits. For each fold, train the hybrid model with differential learning rates: 2e-5 for DistilBERT and 1e-3 for the classifier head and dense encoder. Use AdamW optimizer, cosine learning rate schedule with warmup (10% of steps), gradient clipping (max_norm=1.0), and early stopping on validation log loss with patience=3. Mixed precision training (fp16) for speed. Average predictions across folds and normalize row-wise to sum to 1, then clip to [1e-15, 1-1e-15] before saving submission."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan proposes replacing TF-IDF with DistilBERT to capture contextual semantics, significantly improving log loss from ~0.60 to ~0.35-0.40. Historical experiments confirm that transformer models can achieve lower log loss (as seen in top public kernels), while attempts with TF-IDF+LogisticRegression only reached ~0.47 in the best case, and LightGBM performed worse (~0.79). The current ensemble (LR+LGB) yields ~0.597, indicating that a transformer-based approach is promising. However, a historical experiment with a similar ModernBERT model showed catastrophic overfitting (NaNs), likely due to the small dataset size and improper training setup. To avoid this, the refinement will use DistilBERT (smaller, more stable) with longer training (10 epochs), a smaller batch size (16), mixed precision (FP16), and an 85/15 stratified split for better generalization. No additional feature engineering is needed, as raw text with tokenization is sufficient. Thus, 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' are modified to implement a robust transformer pipeline, while 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' is omitted because the TF-IDF and cleaning steps are replaced by the tokenizer.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the ensemble of LogisticRegression and LightGBM with a DistilBERT-based sequence classifier. Use 'distilbert-base-uncased' from HuggingFace with a linear classification head on the [CLS] token. Remove TF-IDF and text cleaning; instead, tokenize raw text with the DistilBERT tokenizer (max_length=128, truncation/padding). Preserve variable names: 'model' for the PyTorch model, 'tokenizer' for the HuggingFace tokenizer.", "training_evaluation": "Train for 10 epochs using AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01) with a linear warmup scheduler (first 10% steps). Use batch size 16, mixed precision (FP16 via AMP), and gradient accumulation if needed to fit memory. Validate on a fixed 85/15 stratified split (random_state=42). Save best checkpoint based on validation log loss. Generate test predictions with the best model. Preserve: train/val split logic, log loss computation, submission format, and file saving path."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan proposes replacing TF-IDF with DistilBERT to capture contextual semantics, significantly improving log loss from ~0.60 to ~0.35-0.40. Historical experiments confirm that transformer models can achieve lower log loss (as seen in top public kernels), while attempts with TF-IDF+LogisticRegression only reached ~0.47 in the best case, and LightGBM performed worse (~0.79). The current ensemble (LR+LGB) yields ~0.597, indicating that a transformer-based approach is promising. However, a historical experiment with a similar ModernBERT model showed catastrophic overfitting (NaNs), likely due to the small dataset size and improper training setup. To avoid this, the refinement will use DistilBERT (smaller, more stable) with longer training (10 epochs), a smaller batch size (16), mixed precision (FP16), and an 85/15 stratified split for better generalization. No additional feature engineering is needed, as raw text with tokenization is sufficient. Thus, 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' are modified to implement a robust transformer pipeline, while 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' is omitted because the TF-IDF and cleaning steps are replaced by the tokenizer.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the ensemble of LogisticRegression and LightGBM with a DistilBERT-based sequence classifier. Use 'distilbert-base-uncased' from HuggingFace with a linear classification head on the [CLS] token. Remove TF-IDF and text cleaning; instead, tokenize raw text with the DistilBERT tokenizer (max_length=128, truncation/padding). Preserve variable names: 'model' for the PyTorch model, 'tokenizer' for the HuggingFace tokenizer.",
    "training_evaluation": "Train for 10 epochs using AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5, weight_decay=0.01) with a linear warmup scheduler (first 10% steps). Use batch size 16, mixed precision (FP16 via AMP), and gradient accumulation if needed to fit memory. Validate on a fixed 85/15 stratified split (random_state=42). Save best checkpoint based on validation log loss. Generate test predictions with the best model. Preserve: train/val split logic, log loss computation, submission format, and file saving path."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm overfitting: training loss drops rapidly while validation loss plateaus or rises, and fold variance is high (0.3357-0.3434). Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) and Mixup are the core improvements from the initial plan, targeting different aspects of overfitting. MSD reduces effective head capacity, Mixup smooths feature boundaries. Historically, similar experiments with ModernBERT on small datasets showed severe overfitting, and simpler TF-IDF + LogisticRegression worked better but would deviate from the initial plan. Therefore, I choose 'model_design' for MSD (classification head modification) and 'training_evaluation' for Mixup (loss computation and data blending) as the most promising and consistent modules. Data processing is kept unchanged to preserve fold integrity.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "WHAT: Replace the single forward pass in the classification head with Multi-Sample Dropout (K=8). Implement a custom forward method that extracts pooled hidden states from ModernBERT, passes them through a dropout layer and classification head K times, and averages the logits. WHY: MSD forces the classifier to be consistent across multiple dropout masks, reducing overfitting of the randomly initialized head on small data (17k). Historical experiments showed that increasing dropout alone (from 0.1 to 0.3) was insufficient, and MSD provides stronger regularization without changing backbone capacity. HOW: In the model's forward hook, after backbone returns pooled_output, define a small helper loop that runs pooled_output through nn.Dropout(config.hidden_dropout_prob) then through classifier.weight/classifier.bias to get K logit vectors, average them, and return the averaged logits. Ensure the original forward return format (SequenceClassifierOutput) is preserved by overriding the forward method or using a wrapper.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement text-level Mixup augmentation within the training loop. For each batch, sample a mixing coefficient lambda from Beta(0.5,0.5), shuffle the input_ids and attention_mask of the batch, interpolate input_ids and attention_mask of original and shuffled batches, and create mixed labels by interpolating one-hot encoded labels. Compute loss as lambda * CrossEntropyLoss(output, original_labels) + (1-lambda) * CrossEntropyLoss(output, shuffled_labels). WHY: Mixup creates convex combinations of training examples, forcing the model to learn smooth decision boundaries and reducing sensitivity to spurious patterns. Historical experiments with similar small text datasets showed that Mixup consistently improved generalization and reduced fold variance. HOW: In the training loop, before forward pass, sample lambda per batch. Shuffle batch indices to get a permuted version of the same batch. Create mixed input_ids by rounding(lambda * original_input_ids + (1-lambda) * permuted_input_ids) and similar for attention_mask. Compute mixed labels as lambda * one_hot(original_labels) + (1-lambda) * one_hot(permuted_labels). Compute loss as CrossEntropyLoss with the mixed labels (which are soft targets). Keep label smoothing=0.1 applied within the loss function. Use the original model's forward pass with mixed inputs."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm overfitting: training loss drops rapidly while validation loss plateaus or rises, and fold variance is high (0.3357-0.3434). Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) and Mixup are the core improvements from the initial plan, targeting different aspects of overfitting. MSD reduces effective head capacity, Mixup smooths feature boundaries. Historically, similar experiments with ModernBERT on small datasets showed severe overfitting, and simpler TF-IDF + LogisticRegression worked better but would deviate from the initial plan. Therefore, I choose 'model_design' for MSD (classification head modification) and 'training_evaluation' for Mixup (loss computation and data blending) as the most promising and consistent modules. Data processing is kept unchanged to preserve fold integrity.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the single forward pass in the classification head with Multi-Sample Dropout (K=8). Implement a custom forward method that extracts pooled hidden states from ModernBERT, passes them through a dropout layer and classification head K times, and averages the logits. WHY: MSD forces the classifier to be consistent across multiple dropout masks, reducing overfitting of the randomly initialized head on small data (17k). Historical experiments showed that increasing dropout alone (from 0.1 to 0.3) was insufficient, and MSD provides stronger regularization without changing backbone capacity. HOW: In the model's forward hook, after backbone returns pooled_output, define a small helper loop that runs pooled_output through nn.Dropout(config.hidden_dropout_prob) then through classifier.weight/classifier.bias to get K logit vectors, average them, and return the averaged logits. Ensure the original forward return format (SequenceClassifierOutput) is preserved by overriding the forward method or using a wrapper.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement text-level Mixup augmentation within the training loop. For each batch, sample a mixing coefficient lambda from Beta(0.5,0.5), shuffle the input_ids and attention_mask of the batch, interpolate input_ids and attention_mask of original and shuffled batches, and create mixed labels by interpolating one-hot encoded labels. Compute loss as lambda * CrossEntropyLoss(output, original_labels) + (1-lambda) * CrossEntropyLoss(output, shuffled_labels). WHY: Mixup creates convex combinations of training examples, forcing the model to learn smooth decision boundaries and reducing sensitivity to spurious patterns. Historical experiments with similar small text datasets showed that Mixup consistently improved generalization and reduced fold variance. HOW: In the training loop, before forward pass, sample lambda per batch. Shuffle batch indices to get a permuted version of the same batch. Create mixed input_ids by rounding(lambda * original_input_ids + (1-lambda) * permuted_input_ids) and similar for attention_mask. Compute mixed labels as lambda * one_hot(original_labels) + (1-lambda) * one_hot(permuted_labels). Compute loss as CrossEntropyLoss with the mixed labels (which are soft targets). Keep label smoothing=0.1 applied within the loss function. Use the original model's forward pass with mixed inputs."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows validation log-loss plateauing at 0.595 after epoch 8, with overfitting (training loss still decreasing). The initial plan targets optimization (learning rate schedule, optimizer) and regularization (stochastic depth). Based on historical successes, using TF-IDF + LR worked when neural nets overfitted, but here the deep learning approach is core. The historical failure shows that small batch sizes with drop_last=True can cause issues; we already have drop_last=True. The main improvements are in training_evaluation and model_design. Specifically, the learning rate schedule and optimizer changes are in training_evaluation; stochastic depth regularization is in model_design. Data processing remains unchanged as it is already comprehensive and similar to successful TF-IDF approaches. Thus, I focus on model_design (add stochastic depth) and training_evaluation (new LR schedule, NAdamW optimizer, gradient centralization) as per the plan.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Add stochastic depth (drop path) regularization to the ModernBERT backbone by modifying the forward pass to randomly drop entire layers during training with a linear drop rate from 0 to 0.1 across layers. Implement by creating a new class that wraps the pretrained model and applies a Bernoulli mask to skip layers with probability increasing linearly from 0 at layer 0 to 0.1 at the final layer. This acts as a strong regularizer on the trainable top layers, reducing overfitting. Ensure the modification does not change input/output interfaces (still returns logits). Disable during evaluation by setting a self.training flag.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current cosine schedule with a linear warmup for 5 epochs followed by cosine annealing with hard restarts every 3 epochs. Use a lower peak learning rate for the ModernBERT backbone (1e-5) and a higher minimum LR (0.1x peak). Replace AdamW with NAdamW (AdamW with Nesterov momentum) and add gradient centralization by hooking after backward to subtract the mean of gradients across each parameter tensor. This improves training stability and helps escape sharp minima. Keep the same loss function, batch size, and other settings. The scheduler should be computed based on steps per epoch; ensure the code correctly handles restarts by resetting the scheduler state every 3 epochs."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows validation log-loss plateauing at 0.595 after epoch 8, with overfitting (training loss still decreasing). The initial plan targets optimization (learning rate schedule, optimizer) and regularization (stochastic depth). Based on historical successes, using TF-IDF + LR worked when neural nets overfitted, but here the deep learning approach is core. The historical failure shows that small batch sizes with drop_last=True can cause issues; we already have drop_last=True. The main improvements are in training_evaluation and model_design. Specifically, the learning rate schedule and optimizer changes are in training_evaluation; stochastic depth regularization is in model_design. Data processing remains unchanged as it is already comprehensive and similar to successful TF-IDF approaches. Thus, I focus on model_design (add stochastic depth) and training_evaluation (new LR schedule, NAdamW optimizer, gradient centralization) as per the plan.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Add stochastic depth (drop path) regularization to the ModernBERT backbone by modifying the forward pass to randomly drop entire layers during training with a linear drop rate from 0 to 0.1 across layers. Implement by creating a new class that wraps the pretrained model and applies a Bernoulli mask to skip layers with probability increasing linearly from 0 at layer 0 to 0.1 at the final layer. This acts as a strong regularizer on the trainable top layers, reducing overfitting. Ensure the modification does not change input/output interfaces (still returns logits). Disable during evaluation by setting a self.training flag.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current cosine schedule with a linear warmup for 5 epochs followed by cosine annealing with hard restarts every 3 epochs. Use a lower peak learning rate for the ModernBERT backbone (1e-5) and a higher minimum LR (0.1x peak). Replace AdamW with NAdamW (AdamW with Nesterov momentum) and add gradient centralization by hooking after backward to subtract the mean of gradients across each parameter tensor. This improves training stability and helps escape sharp minima. Keep the same loss function, batch size, and other settings. The scheduler should be computed based on steps per epoch; ensure the code correctly handles restarts by resetting the scheduler state every 3 epochs."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting: train loss drops to 0.004 while validation loss increases after epoch 3, reaching 0.66. Historical experiments show that switching to a simpler model with TF-IDF features effectively prevented overfitting on small datasets, and label smoothing with early stopping has been successful in similar tasks. Therefore, I focus on model_design (to incorporate TF-IDF features and a robust classifier) and training_evaluation (to implement early stopping and label smoothing). Adding data_processing_and_feature_engineering is essential because the historical plan's success relied on engineered features (character counts, punctuation, etc.) combined with TF-IDF. This directly addresses the overfitting while preserving the deep learning core where appropriate.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Extract TF-IDF features (1-3 n-grams, max 50000 features) from text using TfidfVectorizer. Also engineer statistical features: character count, word count, sentence count, punctuation count, capital ratio, unique word ratio, stopword ratio, quote density. Standardize numeric features with StandardScaler and combine with TF-IDF via hstack (sparse matrix). Preserve original id and author columns. This follows the successful historical plan that used similar features with a calibrated classifier to reduce overfitting.", "model_design": "Replace the DistilBERT model with a multinomial Logistic Regression classifier (C=4.0, solver='saga', max_iter=1000) trained on the combined TF-IDF + statistical features. For probabilistic calibration, use CalibratedClassifierCV with 'sigmoid' method and 5-fold cross-validation. This simpler model avoids the severe overfitting seen with DistilBERT and aligns with the successful historical experiment where TF-IDF + Logistic Regression achieved stable log loss.", "training_evaluation": "Implement manual 5-fold StratifiedKFold (already in code) but do NOT use early stopping in deep learning sense; instead, train one model per fold with fixed parameters (no epochs). Average fold predictions for test set. Use the same log loss metric with clipping (1e-15) and row normalization. This avoids the overfitting problem entirely by using a simple, well-calibrated model. Ensure submission file matches sample_submission.csv exactly with columns: id, EAP, HPL, MWS."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting: train loss drops to 0.004 while validation loss increases after epoch 3, reaching 0.66. Historical experiments show that switching to a simpler model with TF-IDF features effectively prevented overfitting on small datasets, and label smoothing with early stopping has been successful in similar tasks. Therefore, I focus on model_design (to incorporate TF-IDF features and a robust classifier) and training_evaluation (to implement early stopping and label smoothing). Adding data_processing_and_feature_engineering is essential because the historical plan's success relied on engineered features (character counts, punctuation, etc.) combined with TF-IDF. This directly addresses the overfitting while preserving the deep learning core where appropriate.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
